# Real config holds your Wi-Fi password — never commit it.
#
# The glob covers a copy kept under a different name (.env, wifi.env, home.env)
# as well as the documented one. config.env.example is unaffected: its name
# ends in .example, not .env.
config.env
*.env

# The backup auto-setup.sh writes before it overwrites the config. It is a copy
# of the file above and holds the same password, and it is matched by neither
# pattern: not `config.env`, which is exact, and not `*.env`, because the name
# ends in `.bak`.
#
# Both spellings, and the glob is the one that matters. auto-setup.sh writes
# "$CONFIG_FILE.bak" for whatever RECONNECT_CONFIG names, so a config kept under
# a different name -- exactly the case `*.env` above exists for -- had its
# backup left untracked but not ignored, holding the Wi-Fi password in
# plaintext, with nothing on screen to say so: the run reports "Saved the
# previous config as ...bak" and exits 0. `wifi.env` was ignored and
# `wifi.env.bak` was not.
#
# Deliberately `*.env.bak` rather than `*.bak`: this matches only the backup of
# a config, and a stray editor backup of some other file stays something this
# project has no opinion about. The exact name is kept beside it because it is
# the one the documentation names.
config.env.bak
*.env.bak

# Daily log files produced by reconnect.sh (keep the folder, ignore its logs).
# The .gitkeep is what actually keeps the folder: git tracks files, not
# directories, so without it an empty logs/ would not survive a clone. It needs
# no `!` rule of its own -- nothing in this file matches it. There used to be
# one, which read as though the file were being rescued from an ignore rule and
# invited a later edit to broaden the rule above on the assumption that the
# negation had it covered.
#
# Matched by name rather than by path. RECONNECT_LOG_DIR can send the logs
# anywhere -- install-cron.sh insists on an absolute path, but an absolute path
# may still be inside the checkout, and a hand-run is not restricted at all --
# and `logs/*.log` covered exactly one directory of the several they can land
# in. Nothing in this project tracks a .log file, so the bare pattern costs
# nothing.
*.log

# Lock file reconnect.sh uses to keep two runs from overlapping. Matched by name
# for exactly the reason the daily logs above are: reconnect.sh writes it into
# RECONNECT_LOG_DIR, so it lands wherever they land, and `logs/.lock` covered one
# of the several directories it can land in. Nothing here tracks a file called
# .lock, so the bare pattern costs nothing.
.lock

# OS and editor cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Working notes from the pre-open-source audit; not project documentation.
# LOG.md and VERIFY.md are the same kind of thing: a per-session record of what
# was changed and a per-round record of what was re-checked, useful while the
# tree is being worked on and meaningless to anyone reading the published
# repository -- VERIFY.md cites F-numbers that resolve only against FINDINGS.md,
# which is ignored just above it.
#
# Bare names, matched at any depth for the reason `*.log` and `.lock` above are:
# FINDINGS.md and VERIFY.md live under tests/, and a `tests/` prefix would stop
# matching the day a round puts them anywhere else.
#
# tests/DECISIONS.md is deliberately NOT here. It is project documentation:
# README, CONTRIBUTING and the pull-request template all link to it, and
# reconnect.sh, uninstall-cron.sh and the suite cite its entries by number.
FINDINGS.md
LOG.md
VERIFY.md
